        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Social Darwinism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1857 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Thinkers</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-spencer">Herbert Spencer</a>, its chief developer, whose evolutionary theory was neo-Lamarckian; Thomas Malthus, whose 1798 population essay Spencer's work revived; Francis Galton, founder of eugenics; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernst-haeckel">Ernst Haeckel</a>, who ranked nine human species; William Graham Sumner, its American exponent; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yan-fu">Yan Fu</a>, who brought it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>; Alfred Rosenberg, a key proponent in Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Key texts</span><span class="info-stat-val">Spencer's Progress: Its Law and Cause (1857), First Principles (1860) and The Social Organism (1860); Malthus's An Essay on the Principle of Population (1798); Galton's writings of 1865 and 1869; Sumner's What Social Classes Owe to Each Other (1883); Benjamin Kidd's Social Evolution (1894); Alexander Tille's Entwicklungsethik (1895); Richard Hofstadter's Social Darwinism in American Thought (1944)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Core ideas</span><span class="info-stat-val">the strong should gain wealth and power and the weak lose them; struggle between individuals under laissez-faire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>; struggle between nations or races; eugenics and state-directed breeding; imperial expansion as a law of nature; opposition to aid for the poor; society as an organism that evolves; hereditary mental qualities</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">Scientists generally consider it discredited as a theoretical framework, but it persists within popular culture; the term itself is used mostly by opponents, and scholars dispute whether it names any single coherent ideology</span></div>
