    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Social Darwinism claimed to apply biological evolution and survival of the fittest to society, but its leading exponent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-spencer">Herbert Spencer</a>, held neo-Lamarckian theories that predated and contradicted Darwin's.</li><li>Its forms ranged from defences of laissez-faire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> to racial and national struggle, eugenics, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> and fascism, so that The Concise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> Dictionary of Politics notes a social Darwinist could as well defend state socialism as laissez-faire.</li><li>Scholars link it to the Young Turks' campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and to Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, including Aktion T4; its claims were largely discredited by the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, partly through that association and the scientific consensus against eugenics and scientific racism.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The phrase survival of the fittest was coined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-spencer">Herbert Spencer</a>, not Darwin, and Spencer was not described as a social Darwinist until the 1930s, long after his death; Richard Hofstadter made the term a standard shorthand in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1944.</p></aside>
    </section>

